.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
installs and manages
.Ox
virtual machines under QEMU.
It downloads the install media, creates the disk, answers the installer
over the serial console, and caches the installed disk.
It then drives the lifecycle of the guest: boot, wait, ssh, file
transfer, snapshot, and shutdown.
One command takes a project from nothing to a guest that accepts
.Xr ssh 1 ,
and a second run of that command changes nothing.
.Pp
The host does not have to run
.Ox .
A Linux or Darwin host that runs QEMU is sufficient, and
.Nm
selects the accelerator to match the host.
.Pp
A project is a directory that holds a
.Pa .fuguvmrc
file and a
.Pa .fuguvm/
directory.
Without
.Fl Fl project ,
.Nm
searches up from the current directory for the file.
The directory that holds it is the project root.
See
.Sx CONFIGURATION .
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl Fl emulate
Run the guest under TCG software emulation, whatever the host can do.
.It Fl h , Fl Fl help
Print the usage message and exit.
.It Fl Fl project Ar dir
Use
.Ar dir
as the project root, and do not search for one.
.It Fl q , Fl Fl quiet
Do not print informational messages.
The data lines of
.Cm status ,
.Cm disk info ,
.Cm image export ,
.Cm mirror fetch
and
.Cm snapshot list Fl Fl names
still print.
.It Fl v , Fl Fl verbose
Print debug messages as well.
.It Fl Fl vm Ar name
Operate on the named guest.
The default is the
.Ic default_vm
setting, or
.Cm default .
.El
.Pp
The commands are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm init Op Ar dir
Initialize a project in
.Ar dir ,
which defaults to the current directory.
The command writes a
.Pa .fuguvmrc
with the three project settings, a
.Pa .fuguvm/
directory with the
.Pa vms/
and
.Pa state/
subdirectories, and the declaration of the
.Cm default
guest.
It does nothing when the project is initialized already.
.It Cm up Op Fl Fl no-cache
Make sure that the guest runs.
The command restores the disk from the image cache, or downloads the
install media and installs the guest, and then starts it.
It installs the
.Ic ssh_pubkey
when the guest does not hold it yet.
See
.Sx INSTALLATION .
.Pp
.Fl Fl no-cache
skips both the restore and the save of the image cache for this run,
and overrides the
.Ic image_cache
setting.
The guest then installs from scratch onto a standalone disk.
.It Cm down
Stop the mirror proxy and the guest, and record a clean shutdown.
.It Cm destroy
Stop the guest and delete its disk and state.
.It Cm start
Start the guest in the background.
The command needs an existing disk, refuses a running guest with
status 5, and does not start the proxy.
.It Cm stop Op Fl f , Fl Fl force
Stop the guest.
The shutdown syncs the guest filesystems, powers the guest off through
ACPI, and forces the process to stop only after that.
Every step is bounded.
With
.Fl f ,
the command kills the process at once.
.It Cm status Op Ar key
Print the state of the guest to standard output, as one
.Ar key : value
line for each key, in sorted key order.
The keys are
.Cm accel , arch , bind_address , console_port , disk_exists ,
.Cm installed , name , pid , proxy_url , ssh_port
and
.Cm state .
An empty value means
.Dq not now :
a stopped guest has no
.Cm pid ,
no
.Cm proxy_url ,
and no port for a directive of
.Cm auto .
.Pp
With
.Ar key ,
print the bare value of that one key.
An unknown key exits with status 2, and the message names every key.
.It Cm wait Op Fl Fl timeout Ar seconds
Block until the guest accepts an SSH connection.
The default timeout is 120 seconds, and a timeout exits with status 7.
.It Cm ssh Oo Fl Fl Oc Op Ar command Op Ar argument ...
Open an interactive SSH session to the guest as root, or run one
remote command and exit with its exit status.
The connection authenticates with the SSH agent.
.Pp
Each argument is one word of the remote argument vector.
The tool quotes every word, so the remote shell splits nothing,
expands nothing, and globs nothing.
Write
.Fl Fl
before a word that starts with a hyphen.
A caller that wants a remote shell asks for one:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ fuguvm ssh -- sh -c 'cd /src && make regress'
.Ed
.Pp
The remote command gets no terminal and no standard input.
.It Cm put Oo Fl Fl mode Ar octal Oc Ar local remote
Copy the local file or directory
.Ar local
into the guest at the absolute path
.Ar remote ,
over SFTP.
.Ar remote
is never a container:
.Ql put src /root
writes the content of
.Pa src
into
.Pa /root .
.Pp
The tool writes each file to a temporary name beside its destination
and then publishes every file with
.Xr mv 1 ,
so a failure leaves no partial file.
A directory copy is not atomic as a whole.
.Fl Fl mode
sets the mode of each written file, as 3 or 4 octal digits.
Without it, each file keeps its local permission bits masked with
0777.
The tool refuses a symbolic link, a device node, a socket, a fifo, and
a file above 64 MiB, before it writes one byte.
.It Cm get Ar remote local
Copy one regular guest file at the absolute path
.Ar remote
to the host file
.Ar local .
The tool reads the whole file, at most 64 MiB, and then writes
.Ar local
atomically with mode 0644.
.Ar local
must not be an existing directory.
.It Cm console
Attach the terminal to the serial console of the guest with
.Xr telnet 1 .
End the session with the telnet escape key,
.Ql Ctrl-\&] ,
and then
.Ic quit .
The tool restores the terminal on every exit path.
The console takes one client, so do not attach while an installation
or an
.Cm expect
script runs.
.It Cm expect Ar script Op Ar argument ...
Run an
.Xr expect 1
script against the serial console.
.Ar script
is a path, or the name of a script under
.Pa share/fuguvm/expect/ .
The script gets the host, the port, and each
.Ar argument ,
in that order.
A shipped script reads its timeout from
.Ev FUGUVM_TIMEOUT .
A script that fails exits with status 9.
.It Cm cache list
List the image cache and the mirror cache.
The command prints one line for each cached image, with its key, size,
creation time, and snapshot count, and marks the entry that the
invoked guest derives.
It then prints the mirror downloads by
.Ox
version, and one
.Ql Distfiles
line while the distfile tree holds a file.
.It Cm cache clear Op Fl Fl stale
Remove every cached image and every mirror download, and the temporary
trees that an interrupted save left behind.
With
.Fl Fl stale ,
keep the image that the invoked guest derives and the downloads of the
.Ox
version that it installs.
The flag also keeps the distfile tree and re-applies the
.Ic distfile_cache
cap.
.Pp
The scope is one guest, so a removal for one guest removes the images
of another guest with a different
.Ic version
or
.Ic disk_size .
The command refuses to remove an image that a running guest of the
project uses, with status 5.
It warns when a removal orphans the disk of a stopped guest.
.It Cm snapshot Cm save | restore | rm Ar name
.It Cm snapshot list Op Fl Fl names
Manage the named snapshot layers of the cache entry that the invoked
guest derives.
See
.Sx IMAGE CACHE .
.Pp
.Cm save
flattens the stopped working disk into the cache as a layer over its
base image.
The guest must be installed on a cached image.
.Cm restore
replaces the working disk with a new overlay on the layer, and reseeds
the recorded state.
It needs no disk and no state, so it runs on a fresh checkout before
the first
.Cm up .
Both refuse a running guest with status 5.
A layer that does not exist exits with status 11, so a script tells
it from a failure:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ fuguvm snapshot restore deps-$hash || provision_from_scratch
.Ed
.Pp
.Cm list
prints one line for each layer on standard error.
With
.Fl Fl names ,
it prints the bare names on standard output.
.It Cm image export Ar path Op Fl Fl format Cm qcow2 | raw
Write the installed base image of the invoked guest as a full-disk
image at
.Ar path .
The source is the base image of the cache entry that backs the working
disk.
A standalone disk exports the working disk itself.
The default format is
.Cm qcow2 ,
and
.Cm raw
writes a sparse raw image.
The command refuses a running guest with status 5, and an existing
.Ar path
with status 1.
The write goes through one temporary sibling and one rename.
The report on standard output follows the
.Cm status
contract, with the keys
.Cm bytes , format , key , path
and
.Cm source .
.It Cm mirror fetch Ar file
Download one file of the release of the invoked guest, verify it under
the release key, store it in the mirror cache, and print the cached
path.
The manifests decide the scope: the release scope when the
.Pa SHA256
of the architecture directory names the file, and the source scope
otherwise.
See
.Sx MIRROR CACHE .
.It Cm mirror verify
Verify every cached file of the version and the architecture of the
invoked guest against the two signed manifests.
The command prints one line for each failed file and one summary line
with the counts of ok, failed, and unknown files.
It removes each failed file, and a cached manifest whose signature
fails.
A file that no proven manifest names is unknown, and the command keeps
it.
The command exits with status 1 when one file failed.
.It Cm disk Cm check | repair | info
Manage the working disk with
.Xr qemu-img 1 .
.Cm check
checks the integrity of the image.
.Cm repair
repairs it, and refuses a running guest.
.Cm info
prints the image report, in the
.Ar key : value
form of
.Cm status .
.It Cm help Op Ar command
Print the usage of the tool, or of one command.
.El
.Sh CONFIGURATION
.Nm
reads two files, both named
.Pa .fuguvmrc :
the global file in the home directory, and the project file at the
project root.
The project file wins.
A setting is one
.Ar name Ar value
or
.Ar name No = Ar value
line.
A guest is a
.Ic vm
block, named by its guest name.
A
.Ql #
starts a comment.
A line that does not parse is an error that names the file and the
line, and the tool exits with status 3.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
cache_dir  ~/.cache/fuguvm
default_vm default

vm "default" {
	arch         arm64
	version      7.8
	memory       2048
	disk_size    8G
	ssh_port     2222
	console_port 4444
}
.Ed
.Pp
The top-level settings are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width "distfile_cache" -offset indent
.It Ic cache_dir
The cache directory.
The default is
.Pa ~/.cache/fuguvm .
.It Ic state_dir
The state directory, in the project file only.
The default is
.Pa .fuguvm/state .
.It Ic default_vm
The guest that
.Fl Fl vm
defaults to.
The default is
.Cm default .
.It Ic ssh_pubkey
The SSH public key that
.Cm up
installs for root.
.It Ic qemu_version
The pinned version of the QEMU binary, as one or more dot-separated
numbers.
Without it the tool checks nothing.
See
.Sx ARCHITECTURE .
.It Ic distfile_cache
The size cap of the distfile tree, as a byte count or a number with a
.Cm K ,
.Cm M
or
.Cm G
suffix.
The default is 0, which turns the distfile cache off.
See
.Sx MIRROR CACHE .
.El
.Pp
The settings of a
.Ic vm
block are as follows.
The first five also stand at the top level of either file, and the
block wins.
.Bl -tag -width "root_password_file" -offset indent
.It Ic bind_address
The host IPv4 address of every forwarded port, in dotted-decimal form.
The default is
.Li 127.0.0.1 .
See
.Sx ADDRESSES AND PORTS .
.It Ic image_cache Cm yes | no
Whether
.Cm up
uses the image cache.
The default is
.Cm yes .
.It Ic verify Cm yes | no
Whether the tool verifies each mirror download under the release key,
and whether the installer verifies its sets.
The default is
.Cm yes .
.It Ic signify_dir
The directory of the signify public keys, before
.Pa /etc/signify/
and the shipped keys.
.It Ic ssh_pubkey
As above.
.It Ic name
The name of the guest in messages and in the disk directory.
The default is the block name.
.It Ic arch Cm arm64 | amd64
The guest architecture.
The default is
.Cm arm64 .
.It Ic version
The
.Ox
release.
The default is 7.8.
.It Ic memory
The memory in megabytes.
The default is 2048.
.It Ic disk_size
The virtual disk size, as
.Xr qemu-img 1
reads it.
The default is 8G.
.It Ic ssh_port , console_port
The forwarded SSH port and the serial console port: a number from 1 to
65535, or
.Cm auto .
The defaults are 2222 and 4444.
.It Ic autoinstall
An
.Xr autoinstall 8
response file.
.It Ic base_disk
An existing full-disk image, qcow2 or raw.
.It Ic root_password_file
A file whose first line is the root password of the guest.
.El
.Pp
Each path value expands a leading tilde and resolves a relative path
against the project root.
A
.Ic distfile_cache
line inside a block does not apply, and a
.Ic qemu_version
line there is an error.
A yes/no value that is neither gives a warning and the default.
.Pp
A guest resolves in this order: a
.Ic vm
block of the project file, a
.Ic vm
block of the global file, and the flat settings file
.Pa .fuguvm/vms/ Ns Ar name Ns Pa .conf .
.Cm init
writes the default guest as such a file.
.Sh INSTALLATION
The directives of a guest select one of three install modes, and a
disk belongs to its mode and its architecture for its whole life.
After a change of either, rebuild the guest with
.Cm destroy
and
.Cm up .
.Pp
Without
.Ic autoinstall
and
.Ic base_disk ,
.Cm up
installs with the shipped
.Xr expect 1
script over the serial console.
It generates a random root password, stores it in the state, and
installs the
.Ic ssh_pubkey
over a password login.
A failed script exits with status 9.
.Pp
With
.Ic autoinstall ,
.Cm up
serves the response file to the guest from a loopback HTTP responder,
and the file answers every installer question.
The responder replaces each
.Ql @PROXY_URL@
token in the file with the guest URL of the mirror proxy.
The tool validates no answer in the file, and QEMU runs with
.Fl no-reboot ,
so the reboot at the end of the install cannot start a second one.
.Pp
With
.Ic base_disk ,
.Cm up
installs nothing.
It publishes the image as one cache entry for the whole project, once
for each host, and overlays the working disk on it.
The tool never writes to the source file.
.Ic autoinstall
and
.Ic base_disk
contradict each other, and
.Ic base_disk
needs
.Ic image_cache yes .
.Pp
Outside the default mode the tool generates no password.
With
.Ic ssh_pubkey
and
.Ic root_password_file ,
.Cm up
reads the password from the file and installs the key over a password
login.
Without
.Ic ssh_pubkey ,
it waits for SSH with the key of the operator, which the image must
trust already.
.Ic ssh_pubkey
without
.Ic root_password_file
is a configuration error.
.Pp
Before the first boot the tool proves the release manifest under the
release key and verifies the miniroot image.
The installer of a numbered release verifies its own sets.
With
.Ic verify yes
the install script therefore stops on the
.Dq Continue without verification?
prompt with status 9, because the prompt reports a broken mirror.
With
.Ic verify no
the tool downloads without a proof and the installer waives its check,
with one warning on each side.
.Sh ARCHITECTURE
The
.Ic arch
directive selects the QEMU binary, the machine type, the firmware, the
installer answers, and the image-cache key.
.Cm up
and
.Cm start
exit with status 3 when the QEMU binary of the architecture is not on
.Ev PATH .
.Pp
The accelerator follows the host.
The tool selects KVM on a Linux host with a writable
.Pa /dev/kvm ,
and HVF on a Darwin host, and only when the host machine runs the
instruction set of the guest.
It selects TCG in every other case, and under
.Fl Fl emulate .
An installation under TCG takes tens of minutes.
.Pp
With
.Ic qemu_version ,
.Cm up
and
.Cm start
run the binary with
.Fl Fl version
before the first spawn and compare component by component, over the
components that the directive names:
.Li 9.0
accepts
.Li 9.0.4
and refuses
.Li 9.1.0 .
A mismatch, an absent binary, and output with no version each exit
with status 3.
.Sh ADDRESSES AND PORTS
QEMU binds the forwarded SSH port and the serial console to
.Ic bind_address .
The default,
.Li 127.0.0.1 ,
lets the host alone reach a guest.
The tool connects to the same address, or to
.Li 127.0.0.1
when the ports bind to
.Li 0.0.0.0 .
.Pp
With
.Cm auto ,
.Cm up
and
.Cm start
take the first free port of a fixed range of 100 ports above the
default port of the directive, and record it.
The probe skips every port that a sibling guest of the project holds
or declares, running or not.
An exhausted range exits with status 1.
The record dies with the run:
.Cm stop
clears it, and a later
.Cm start
resolves again.
Read the port with
.Cm status .
.Pp
.Cm ssh ,
.Cm put ,
.Cm get
and
.Cm console
refuse a stopped guest with status 1.
.Cm expect
and
.Cm wait
refuse a stopped guest with a port of
.Cm auto
with the same status, because such a guest has no port.
.Sh IMAGE CACHE
An installed disk lands in the cache as a clean, compacted base image,
and a later
.Cm up
overlays a throwaway working disk on it.
With a warm cache,
.Cm destroy
and
.Cm up
are a cheap factory reset.
The entries sit under
.Ic cache_dir ,
beside the mirror cache:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
<cache_dir>/proxy/...                               the mirror cache
<cache_dir>/installed/<key>/base.qcow2              mode 0400
<cache_dir>/installed/<key>/meta.json               mode 0600
<cache_dir>/installed/<key>/snapshots/<name>.qcow2  mode 0400
<cache_dir>/installed/<key>/snapshots/<name>.json   mode 0600
.Ed
.Pp
The key is
.Ar version Ns - Ns Ar arch Ns - Ns Ar hash .
The hash covers every input that shapes the installed disk of the
mode: the version, the architecture, the mode, and the file
.Pa share/fuguvm/cache-generation .
The default mode adds
.Ic disk_size
and the content of
.Pa install.exp .
The autoinstall mode adds
.Ic disk_size ,
the content of
.Pa autoinstall.exp ,
and the content of the response file.
The import mode adds nothing, so every guest of a project derives one
key for one
.Ic base_disk .
The memory and port settings do not shape the disk, and a change to
them hits the same entry.
.Pp
The generation file is a counter.
Increase the number when the install procedure changes outside the
scripts, and keep the bare number in the file.
.Pp
An entry is immutable and write-once.
The tool builds it whole in a temporary directory and publishes it with
one rename.
A snapshot is an immutable named layer over the base image of an
entry, and
.Cm snapshot save
flattens the working disk onto the base:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
base.qcow2  <-  <name>.qcow2  <-  disk.qcow2
.Ed
.Pp
No snapshot is the parent of another, so the removal of one orphans
none.
Each operation that invalidates a base also invalidates its snapshots.
.Sh MIRROR CACHE
A proxy on the host caches what an installation and a ports build
fetch from
.Lk https://cdn.openbsd.org :
the file sets, the packages, the syspatch sets, the kernels, the
miniroot images, the manifests, and the four source tarballs of a
release.
With a
.Ic distfile_cache
cap above zero, it also caches the distfile tree under
.Pa /pub/OpenBSD/distfiles/
of any mirror host.
The proxy admits nothing else, and it caches no https body.
The guest reaches it at the
.Cm proxy_url
of
.Cm status ,
and the port changes between runs.
.Pp
Every tree except the distfiles carries a version in its path, and
.Cm cache clear Fl Fl stale
prunes the other versions.
The
.Ic distfile_cache
cap bounds the distfile tree instead: the proxy evicts the oldest file
first after each store.
.Pp
The host verifies each download under the base key of the release with
.Xr signify 1
before the file enters the cache.
The version names the key file: 7.8 gives
.Pa openbsd-78-base.pub .
The tool reads the key from
.Ic signify_dir ,
then from
.Pa /etc/signify/ ,
and then from the shipped
.Pa share/fuguvm/signify/
tree, which holds the key of the default release.
A guest of an older release needs one of the first two.
The tool serves no snapshot release.
Each scope holds one
.Pa SHA256
manifest with one signature: the architecture directory signs the sets,
the kernels and the miniroot, and the version directory signs the
source tarballs.
.Pp
A ports build in the guest needs two lines of guest configuration.
.Ev http_proxy
must name the
.Cm proxy_url
value, because
.Xr ftp 1
in the guest reads it.
The ports tree must fetch each distfile over http from the
.Ox
distfile mirror, because an https site never reaches the cache.
.Va SITE_BACKUP
in
.Xr bsd.port.mk 5
is that route:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ fuguvm ssh -- sh -c \e
	"echo 'SITE_BACKUP = http://ftp.openbsd.org/pub/OpenBSD/distfiles/' \e
	>> /etc/mk.conf"
$ proxy="$(fuguvm status proxy_url)"
$ fuguvm ssh -- sh -c \e
	"cd /usr/ports/devel/gmake && env http_proxy=$proxy make fetch"
.Ed
.Pp
The host verified the ports tree, and
.Ql make checksum
verifies each distfile against its
.Pa distinfo
digest in the guest.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width ".fuguvm/vms/<name>.conf" -compact
.It Pa ~/.fuguvmrc
The global configuration.
.It Pa .fuguvmrc
The project configuration, at the project root.
.It Pa .fuguvm/vms/<name>.conf
A guest declaration outside the two configuration files.
.It Pa .fuguvm/state/<name>/
The state of one guest: the working disk, the JSON state, the PID
files, and the logs.
.It Pa ~/.cache/fuguvm/
The default cache directory.
.It Pa share/fuguvm/expect/
The shipped
.Xr expect 1
scripts.
.It Pa share/fuguvm/cache-generation
The generation counter of the image cache.
.It Pa share/fuguvm/signify/
The signify public key of the default release.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits with one of the following values:
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width Ds -offset indent -compact
.It 0
Success.
.It 1
General error.
A stopped guest on a connection command, a failed transfer, a failed
download or verification, an exhausted port range, and an
.Cm image export
target that exists also give this status.
.It 2
Invalid arguments.
.It 3
Configuration error: no project, a line that does not parse, an
invalid value, a missing or contradicting install directive, an
absent QEMU binary, or a QEMU version that does not match the pin.
.It 4
Guest not found.
.It 5
The guest runs.
.It 7
Timeout in
.Cm wait .
.It 9
An
.Xr expect 1
script failed, the install scripts included.
.It 11
Snapshot not found.
.El
.Pp
.Cm ssh
with a
.Ar command
exits with the status of the remote command.
A remote status can read like a tool status, and the diagnostic on
standard error tells the two apart.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Initialize a project, boot the default guest, and run a command in it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ fuguvm init
$ fuguvm up
$ fuguvm ssh -- uname -a
OpenBSD openbsd-test.my.domain 7.8 GENERIC arm64
.Ed
.Pp
Copy a source tree in, build it, and copy the report back:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ fuguvm put src /root/src
$ fuguvm ssh -- sh -c 'cd /root/src && make regress > report.txt'
$ fuguvm get /root/src/report.txt report.txt
.Ed
.Pp
Declare an amd64 guest and a fleet of two guests with automatic ports
in
.Pa .fuguvmrc :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
vm "openbsd-amd64" {
	arch      amd64
	disk_size 16G
}
vm "scenario-1" {
	ssh_port     auto
	console_port auto
}
vm "scenario-2" {
	ssh_port     auto
	console_port auto
}

$ fuguvm --vm openbsd-amd64 up
$ fuguvm --vm scenario-1 up
$ fuguvm --vm scenario-1 status ssh_port
2222
.Ed
.Pp
Cache a provisioned state, and restore it on the next checkout:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ fuguvm ssh -- pkg_add gmake
$ fuguvm down
$ fuguvm snapshot save deps
$ fuguvm snapshot restore deps && fuguvm up
.Ed
